In <1993May7.201619.2698@ugle.unit.no> hafting@Lise.Unit.NO (Helge Hafting) writes:


>I am trying to change the game a bit in order to allow for different types
>of coins.  I have some questions for those of you that will be interested
>in these changes.

>There will be differences between the different metals, copper may corrode
>(and silver too, if exposed to eggs...) but the main difference is of course
>weight.  There are two ways to implement weight difference, I would like to
>know what people prefer:

>1.  One coin is always worth one zorkmid, regardless of metal.  Copper coins
>    will have to be heavy...  This has the advantage that it is easy to see
>    how much money you have.  E.g. 100 gold + 100 copper = 200 zorkmids

>2.  The method I prefer, as it seems more realistic:
>    Copper is used for small change, so copper coins have a low value.
>    The coins will still have different weights, but copper coins may now
>    be lighter than gold pieces.  (But the same amount of zorkmids will of
>    course still be heavier in copper than in gold.)
>    100 gold + 100 copper in the inventory may add upp to 120 zorkmids or
>    something like that.  This won't be any real problem, as the
>    bottom line of the screen always will show your total amount of zorkmids.
>    (probably rounded down to the nearest zorkmid, I don't want to implement
>     decimal prices.  Adding new objects to the game don't seem to be 
>     difficult, except when it comes to coins...  There is a lot of
>     code to change.)  This is what I will implement, unless the opinion is
>     massively in favor of alternative 1.

>Gold is currently not an object in the inventory, but I will change it to be
>that.  It will be a new category for money.  A few inventory letters will be used
>up, but only one for each coin type.  The advantage of doing this is
>that it will be a lot easier to add new coins in the future, for example
>false money, 100-zorkmid bills (light, but may burn!), or traveller cheques for
>the tourists.

>The amounts of money generated will be exactly the same as before, in order
>to preserve game balance.  

>There are two ways to implement different values, if weight method 2 is used:

>a. A gold piece is worth 1 zorkmid, so gold keeps its traditional value.
>   Coins made of lesser metal will have to have fractional values.  (I will
>   implement it with integers, different platforms may have different views on
>   floating point.)

>b. Normal coins don't have fractional values, a copper piece may have value 1,
>   and a gold piece perhaps 20.  This means fewer gold pieces in the game
>   in order to preserve balance.  I won't rule out the possibility of having
>   fractional coins, perhaps it could be fun with some (almost) useless
>   money types (orcish coins? :-) with a status similar to that of rubels.
>   Instead of being "not interested", a shopkeeper may offer you a few of the
>   lowly coins for unusable items like rocks and cursed -4 darts.

>I have no clear preference for a or b, I'll implement wathever people want.

>It seems to me that mithril is some sort of metal.  (I might be wrong, I haven't
>heard of this stuff anywhere but in nethack.)  Should I implement mithril coins,
>or is that stupid?

>When it comes to metal prices, should I use today's metal prices (what are they,
>anyway?) or should I use "ancient" pricing?  There was a time when 
>aluminium was much more valuable than gold, as it were a new metal.  

>Helge Hafting



